 To allow seeing a float volume through a reference volume, the weight is determined by gradient magnitude $|\nabla|$ [S1]:
 $$ weight = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } |\nabla| < c \\ 1 & \text{else} \end{cases} $$
-where $c$ is a constant [S1]. Gradient magnitude acts as a numerical divergence; surfaces have higher values, while homogeneous regions have lower values. Setting weight to 0 in homogeneous areas makes them transparent, eliminating opacity in overlapping regions [S1].
